Persons who use closed systems to create, modify, maintain or transmit electronic records must employ procedures and controls designed to ensure the authenticity, integrity and, when appropriate, the confidentiality of electronic records, and to ensure that the signer cannot readily repudiate the signed record as not genuine (21 CFR 11.10). Those controls include:

(a) validation of systems to ensure accuracy, reliability, consistent intended performance and the ability to discern invalid or altered records;
(b) the ability to generate accurate and complete copies of records in both human readable and electronic form suitable for inspection, review and copying by the agency;
(c) protection of records to enable their accurate and ready retrieval throughout the records retention period;
(d) limiting system access to authorized individuals;
(e) use of secure, computer-generated, time-stamped audit trails that independently record the date and time of operator entries and actions creating, modifying or deleting records, where record changes must not obscure previously recorded information;
(f) use of operational system checks to enforce permitted sequencing of steps and events, as appropriate;
(g) use of authority checks to ensure that only authorized individuals can use the system, sign a record electronically, access the input or output device, alter a record, or perform the operation at hand;
(h) use of device checks to determine, as appropriate, the validity of the source of data input or operational instruction;
(i) determination that persons who develop, maintain or use the systems have the education, training and experience to perform their assigned tasks;
(j) establishment of, and adherence to, written policies holding individuals accountable and responsible for actions initiated under their electronic signatures, to deter record and signature falsification; and
(k) controls over systems documentation, comprising adequate controls over its distribution, access and use (11.10(k)(1)) and revision and change control procedures maintaining an audit trail of time-sequenced development and modification of that documentation (11.10(k)(2)).

Verification activities under Part 117 must include, as appropriate to the nature of the preventive control and its role in the facility's food safety system, five things (21 CFR 117.155(a)):

1. Validation, in accordance with 117.160 (117.155(a)(1));
2. Verification that monitoring is being conducted as required by 117.140, in accordance with 117.145 (117.155(a)(2));
3. Verification that appropriate decisions about corrective actions are being made as required by 117.140, in accordance with 117.150 (117.155(a)(3));
4. Verification of implementation and effectiveness, in accordance with 117.165 (117.155(a)(4)); and
5. Reanalysis, in accordance with 117.170 (117.155(a)(5)).

All verification activities must be documented in records (117.155(b)).

Validation (117.160) requires validating that the preventive controls identified and implemented under 117.135 are adequate to control the hazard. It must be performed or overseen by a preventive controls qualified individual before implementation of the food safety plan, or where necessary to demonstrate the control measures can be implemented as designed, within 90 calendar days after production of the food first begins or within a reasonable timeframe supported by a written justification; and again whenever a change to a control measure could affect its effectiveness, and whenever a reanalysis reveals the need (117.160(b)(1)). It must include obtaining and evaluating scientific and technical evidence, or conducting studies where such evidence is unavailable or inadequate (117.160(b)(2)). Validation is not required for the food allergen controls in 117.135(c)(2), the sanitation controls in 117.135(c)(3), the recall plan in 117.139, the supply-chain program in subpart G, or other preventive controls where a preventive controls qualified individual prepares a written justification that validation is not applicable (117.160(c)).

Verification of implementation and effectiveness (117.165(a)) requires verifying that preventive controls are consistently implemented and are effectively and significantly minimizing or preventing the hazards, through calibration of process monitoring and verification instruments or checking them for accuracy; product testing for a pathogen, appropriate indicator organism or other hazard; environmental monitoring where contamination of a ready-to-eat food with an environmental pathogen is a hazard requiring a preventive control; and review of monitoring and corrective action records within 7 working days of creation, or a reasonable timeframe with written justification, and of calibration, testing and supplier verification records within a reasonable time (117.165(a)(1) through (4)). Written procedures are required for calibration, product testing and environmental monitoring (117.165(b)).

You must prepare, or have prepared, and implement a written food safety plan, and its preparation must be done or overseen by one or more preventive controls qualified individuals (21 CFR 117.126(a)(1) and (a)(2)).

The written food safety plan must include seven items (117.126(b)):
1. the written hazard analysis required by 117.130(a)(2);
2. the written preventive controls required by 117.135(b);
3. the written supply-chain program required by subpart G of this part;
4. the written recall plan required by 117.139(a);
5. the written procedures for monitoring implementation of the preventive controls required by 117.145(a);
6. the written corrective action procedures required by 117.150(a)(1); and
7. the written verification procedures required by 117.165(b).

The food safety plan is itself a record subject to the recordkeeping requirements of subpart F of this part (117.126(c)).

In adhering to Clause 7.5.4 in ISO 13485, Servicing Activities (incorporated by reference at 21 CFR 820.7), the manufacturer must record at a minimum six items for servicing activities (21 CFR 820.35(b)):

1. the name of the device serviced (820.35(b)(1));
2. any unique device identifier (UDI) or universal product code (UPC), and any other device identification(s) (820.35(b)(2));
3. the date of service (820.35(b)(3));
4. the individual(s) who serviced the device (820.35(b)(4));
5. the service performed (820.35(b)(5)); and
6. any test and inspection data (820.35(b)(6)).

This sits in 820.35, Control of Records, which adds FDA-specified record content on top of Clause 4.2.5 in ISO 13485.

Under 21 CFR 117.10 the management of the establishment must take reasonable measures and precautions to ensure the following.

Disease control (117.10(a)). Any person who, by medical examination or supervisory observation, is shown to have or appears to have an illness, open lesion including boils, sores or infected wounds, or any other abnormal source of microbial contamination presenting a reasonable possibility of contamination, must be excluded from operations that may be expected to result in such contamination until the condition is corrected.

Cleanliness (117.10(b)). All persons working in direct contact with food, food-contact surfaces and food-packaging materials must conform to hygienic practices while on duty to the extent necessary to protect against allergen cross-contact and contamination. The methods include:
- wearing outer garments suitable to the operation (117.10(b)(1));
- maintaining adequate personal cleanliness (117.10(b)(2));
- washing hands thoroughly, and sanitizing if necessary, in an adequate hand-washing facility before starting work, after each absence from the work station, and at any other time when the hands may have become soiled or contaminated (117.10(b)(3));
- removing all unsecured jewelry and other objects that might fall into food, equipment or containers, and removing hand jewelry that cannot be adequately sanitized during periods when food is manipulated by hand, or covering it with material that can be maintained intact, clean and sanitary (117.10(b)(4));
- maintaining gloves, if used in food handling, in an intact, clean and sanitary condition (117.10(b)(5));
- wearing hair nets, headbands, caps, beard covers or other effective hair restraints, where appropriate (117.10(b)(6));
- storing clothing and personal belongings away from areas where food is exposed or equipment and utensils are washed (117.10(b)(7));
- confining eating, chewing gum, drinking beverages and using tobacco to areas other than where food may be exposed or equipment and utensils are washed (117.10(b)(8)); and
- taking any other necessary precautions against allergen cross-contact and contamination with microorganisms or foreign substances (117.10(b)(9)).

Each premarket notification submission must contain the information listed in 21 CFR 807.87:

(a) the device name, including both the trade or proprietary name and the common or usual name or classification name;
(b) the establishment registration number, if applicable, of the owner or operator submitting the notification;
(c) the class in which the device has been put under section 513 of the act and, if known, its appropriate panel; or, where the owner or operator determines the device has not been classified, a statement of that determination and its basis;
(d) action taken to comply with the performance standard requirements of section 514 of the act;
(e) proposed labels, labeling and advertisements sufficient to describe the device, its intended use and the directions for its use, with photographs or engineering drawings where applicable;
(f) a statement indicating how the device is similar to and/or different from other products of comparable type in commercial distribution, accompanied by supporting data, which may identify similar products, materials and design considerations;
(g) where the device has undergone a significant change or modification that could significantly affect safety or effectiveness, or is to be marketed for a new or different indication, the information described above on that change or modification; and
(h) a 510(k) summary as described in 807.92, or a 510(k) statement as described in 807.93.

21 CFR 820.198 is no longer in force - there are no live rows for it, and the former Subparts C through O of Part 820, which contained it, are [Reserved]. So there is no complaint file content specified at 820.198 today.

Complaint record content is now specified at 820.35(a). In addition to Clause 8.2.2 in ISO 13485, Complaint Handling (incorporated by reference at 820.7), the manufacturer must maintain records of the review, evaluation and investigation of any complaint involving the possible failure of a device, labeling or packaging to meet any of its specifications, and must include:
1. the name of the device (820.35(a)(1));
2. the date the complaint was received (820.35(a)(2));
3. any unique device identifier (UDI) or universal product code (UPC), and any other device identification(s) (820.35(a)(3));
4. the name, address and phone number of the complainant (820.35(a)(4));
5. the nature and details of the complaint (820.35(a)(5));
6. any correction or corrective action taken (820.35(a)(6)); and
7. any reply to the complainant (820.35(a)(7)).

So the route is 820.35(a) together with ISO 13485 Clause 8.2.2, not 820.198.

The difference is one of function and independence, and Part 111 states it in two places.

Separation of duties (21 CFR 111.12(b)). You must identify who is responsible for your quality control operations. Each person identified to perform quality control operations must be qualified to do so and must have responsibilities related to performing those operations that are distinct and separate from the responsibilities that person has when not performing them. That is the source of the independence: a person may hold both kinds of duty, but the quality control duties must be distinct and separate, so production work is not approved by the same person acting in the same capacity. Both groups are subject to the same baseline qualification requirement - each person engaged in manufacturing, packaging, labeling or holding, or in performing any quality control operations, must have the education, training or experience to perform the assigned functions (111.12(c)), and you must have qualified employees who manufacture, package, label or hold dietary supplements (111.12(a)).

What quality control personnel do (111.105). Quality control personnel must ensure that manufacturing, packaging, labeling and holding operations ensure the quality of the dietary supplement and that it is packaged and labeled as specified in the master manufacturing record. Their functions are approval and review functions rather than production functions:
- approving or rejecting all processes, specifications, written procedures, controls, tests and examinations, and deviations from or modifications to them, that may affect the identity, purity, strength or composition of a dietary supplement (111.105(a));
- reviewing and approving the documentation setting out the basis for qualification of any supplier (111.105(b));
- reviewing and approving the documentation setting out why meeting in-process specifications, combined with meeting component specifications, will help ensure the identity, purity, strength and composition are met (111.105(c));
- reviewing and approving the documentation setting out why the results of the tests or examinations for each product specification selected under 111.75(c)(1) will ensure the finished batch meets product specifications (111.105(d));
- reviewing and approving the basis and documentation for exempting any product specification from the verification requirements of 111.75(c)(1), and for why other testing, examination or monitoring will ensure the exempted specification is met (111.105(e));
- ensuring that required representative samples are collected (111.105(f)) and that required reserve samples are collected and held (111.105(g));
- determining whether all specifications established under 111.70(a) are met (111.105(h)); and
- performing other operations required under the subpart (111.105(i)).

So production personnel carry out the manufacturing, packaging, labeling and holding operations; quality control personnel approve or reject the processes and specifications governing that work, determine whether specifications are met, and do so under duties that must be distinct and separate from any production responsibilities they hold.

Who the person is. A preventive controls qualified individual means a qualified individual who has successfully completed training in the development and application of risk-based preventive controls at least equivalent to that received under a standardized curriculum recognized as adequate by FDA, or is otherwise qualified through job experience to develop and apply a food safety system (21 CFR 117.3). 117.180(c)(1) states the same requirement and adds that job experience may qualify an individual to perform these functions if that experience has provided knowledge at least equivalent to that provided through the standardized curriculum. All applicable training in the development and application of risk-based preventive controls must be documented in records, including the date of the training, the type of training, and the person(s) trained (117.180(d)).

What the person must do or oversee (117.180(a)). One or more preventive controls qualified individuals must do or oversee:
1. preparation of the food safety plan, under 117.126(a)(2) (117.180(a)(1));
2. validation of the preventive controls, under 117.160(b)(1) (a)(2);
3. the written justification for validation to be performed in a timeframe exceeding the first 90 calendar days of production of the applicable food (a)(3);
4. the determination that validation is not required, under 117.160(c)(5) (a)(4);
5. review of records, under 117.165(a)(4) (a)(5);
6. the written justification for reviewing monitoring and corrective action records in a timeframe exceeding 7 working days (a)(6);
7. reanalysis of the food safety plan, under 117.170(d) (a)(7); and
8. the determination that reanalysis can be completed, and additional preventive controls validated as appropriate, in a timeframe exceeding the first 90 calendar days of production of the applicable food (a)(8).

A related but distinct role in the same section: a qualified auditor must conduct an onsite audit under 117.435(a) (117.180(b)), and to be a qualified auditor a qualified individual must have technical expertise obtained through education, training or experience, or a combination, necessary to perform the auditing function (117.180(c)(2)).

For a single-entity or co-packaged combination product, 21 CFR 4.4(a) provides that compliance with all applicable CGMP requirements must be achieved through the design and implementation of a CGMP operating system that satisfies either the specifics of each set of CGMP regulations listed under 4.3 as they apply to each constituent part (4.4(a)(1)), or the alternative in 4.4(b) (4.4(a)(2)). The second route is the streamlined approach: demonstrate compliance with one system in full, then add the specified provisions of the other rather than operating both regimes.

Drug-based system plus specified device provisions (4.4(b)(1)). Where the product includes a device constituent part and a drug constituent part, and the operating system has been shown to comply with the drug CGMP requirements or the medical gas CGMP requirements as applicable, these must also be satisfied:
- general requirements and management responsibility - ISO 13485 Clause 4.1, Clause 5 and its subclauses, Clause 6.1, and 820.10 (4.4(b)(1)(i));
- design and development - ISO 13485 Clause 7.3 and its subclauses, with one or more documented processes for risk management in product realization and records of risk management activities maintained (b)(1)(ii));
- purchasing - Clause 7.4 and its subclauses (b)(1)(iii));
- analysis of data, improvement and complaint handling - Clause 8.2.2 and 820.35(a), Clause 8.4, and Clause 8.5 and its subclauses (b)(1)(iv));
- installation activities - Clause 7.5.3 (b)(1)(v)); and
- servicing activities - Clause 7.5.4 and 820.35(b) (b)(1)(vi)).

Device-based system plus specified drug provisions (4.4(b)(2)). Where the product includes a device constituent part and a drug constituent part other than a medical gas, and the operating system has been shown to comply with the QMSR requirements for devices, these drug CGMP provisions must also be satisfied: 211.84 testing and approval or rejection of components, containers and closures; 211.103 calculation of yield; 211.132 tamper-evident packaging for OTC human drug products; 211.137 expiration dating; 211.165 testing and release for distribution; 211.166 stability testing; 211.167 special testing requirements; and 211.170 reserve samples.

Medical gas variant (4.4(b)(3)). Where the drug constituent part is a medical gas and the operating system complies with the QMSR, the additional provisions are 213.84 testing and approval or rejection of components, containers and closures; 213.94 medical gas containers and closures; 213.122 materials examination and usage criteria; and 213.165 testing and release for distribution.

So the streamlined approach is a choice of base system plus a defined, closed list of additions from the other - not a free blend, and not an exemption from either regime.

Which actions are exempt from reporting (21 CFR 806.1(b)). Four categories are exempt from the reporting requirements of Part 806:
1. actions taken by device manufacturers or importers to improve the performance or quality of a device but that do not reduce a risk to health posed by the device or remedy a violation of the act caused by the device (806.1(b)(1));
2. market withdrawal as defined in 806.2(i) (806.1(b)(2));
3. routine servicing as defined in 806.2(l) (806.1(b)(3)); and
4. stock recovery as defined in 806.2(m) (806.1(b)(4)).

There is also a separate route out of a report under 806.10(a)(2): a correction or removal initiated to remedy a violation of the act which may present a risk to health need not be reported where the information has already been provided as set out in 806.10(f), or where the action is exempt under 806.1(b).

What records are still required (806.20). A manufacturer or importer who initiates a correction or removal that is not required to be reported under 806.10 must keep a record of it (806.20(a)). The record must contain:
- the brand name, common or usual name, classification, name and product code if known, and the intended use of the device (806.20(b)(1));
- the UDI, or the device identifier, UPC, model, catalog or code number, and the manufacturing lot or serial number or other identification number (b)(2));
- a description of the events giving rise to the information and the corrective or removal action taken and expected to be taken (b)(3));
- the justification for not reporting the action to FDA, which must contain conclusions and any follow-ups and be reviewed and evaluated by a designated person (b)(4)); and
- a copy of all communications regarding the correction or removal (b)(5)).

Retention. Records required under this section must be retained for 2 years beyond the expected life of the device, even if the manufacturer or importer has ceased to manufacture or import it (806.20(c)).

The justification requirement in 806.20(b)(4) is the point of the distinction: an exempt action is not an unrecorded action, and the decision not to report must itself be documented, reasoned and reviewed.
